vendor/hyperframes/hyperframes/references/motion.md:8:- **Render-time blur**: for fast 2D movers, `data-motion-blur="on"` on the composition root smears them along their real path in preview and render (opt-in; also `data-motion-blur-shutter` for a longer smear, 360 is strong; `-events="on"` when the motion is set from a timeline callback such as a `render(t)` function). Suggest it when the person asks for blur or a filmic look and the piece moves fast. It draws with a per-element filter that works over WebGL and in any Chrome (cost varies by film; on two real films it cost more than capture) (spins and zooms soften rather than streak); `data-motion-blur-tier="capture"` opts into the truer capture, which loses anything in front of a mover and ignores blend modes. It is off only for HDR and distributed renders. Opt a subtree out with `data-motion-blur="off"`, e.g. a seam that already carries its own blur. After adding it run `hyperframes check`. Guide: /guides/motion-blur.
